<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Partitions of Poland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Partitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> were three divisions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1772, 1793 and 1795. The first took about a third of its territory; the second and third followed attempts at reform and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosciuszko-uprising">Kościuszko Uprising</a>, and ended the state altogether. Prussia gained Royal Prussia, Danzig, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. Poland did not regain independence until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/partitions-of-poland.jpg" alt="Partitions of Poland" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathiasrex Maciej Szczepańczyk, based on layers of Halibutt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polish-Lithuanian_Commonwealth_in_1772.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the mid-18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> was in disorder. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberum-veto">liberum veto</a>, developed in the reign of Władysław IV, let a single deputy strike down an act, and foreign diplomats bribed nobles to use it. In 1730 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> agreed in secret to keep the commonwealth's laws unchanged. At the Repnin Sejm of 1767 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a>'s ambassador forced a constitution on the commonwealth that guaranteed the liberum veto and undid earlier reforms. The resentment this caused led to the War of the Confederation of Bar of 1768 to 1772, in which poorly commanded Polish forces were defeated by the regular Russian army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition</a> was agreed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in February 1772, and the occupation manifesto was issued on 5 August 1772. The commonwealth lost about 30 per cent of its territory and half its population, about four million people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> took most of Royal Prussia, Ermland and the Netze District, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> with the salt mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bochnia">Bochnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wieliczka">Wieliczka</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> took lands east of the Dvina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislavl">Mstislavl</a>. The so-called Partition Sejm approved the cession on 18 September 1773 under Russian military threat.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a> enfranchised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> and separated the branches of government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> invaded in 1792, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> agreed with Russia to take more land. At the Grodno Sejm, in the presence of Russian forces, deputies accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a>, signed on 23 January 1793, which left only one third of the 1772 population in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosciuszko-uprising">Kościuszko Uprising</a> of 1794 failed, and on 24 October 1795 the three powers divided what remained. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski">Stanisław August Poniatowski</a> abdicated at Grodno on 25 November 1795.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The commonwealth was eliminated for 123 years. By the Third Partition Russia held 45 per cent of the commonwealth's population, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> 32 per cent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> 23 per cent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> set up the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1807, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 Russia created the Congress Kingdom of Poland. Under Russian rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> suffered confiscation of property, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> and the closure of their universities after the uprising of 1831. In the Austrian sector, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, Poles had representation in parliament and their own universities. Polish poets and politicians went into emigration, and Polish legions fought alongside Napoleon. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> allowed full independence to be restored in 1918.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Partitions of Poland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 1772 and 1795 Prussia, the Austrian Empire and the Russian Empire divided the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth in three <b>Partitions of Poland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prussia’s rise turned German politics into a contest between two great powers, Austria and Prussia, and its seizure of Silesia and share in the three <b>Partitions of Poland</b> (1772, 1793 and 1795) redrew the map of central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Commonwealth’s last years royal elections had come to be seen as a source of conflict and a symbol of anarchy, and many historians have counted them among the causes of the weakness that ended in the <b>Partitions of Poland</b> between 1772 and 1795.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
